  "response": "**Setup**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(−|¬D) = 0.95 → P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Bayes' theorem**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)P(D) + P(+|¬D)P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98)(0.002) + (0.05)(0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.04990\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = P(+|D)P(D) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 = 0.0377949...\n\n**Result**\n\n≈ 3.78%\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly sensitive/specific test, when the underlying condition is rare, most positive results are false positives — here roughly 96% of people who test positive don't actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
